|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_sqrt_xy_construction_

    Given two lengths $A$ and $B$, how can
    $$
    \sqrt{AB}
    $$
    be constructed using
    only ruler and compass?

    |> Solution

        A preliminary “arithmetic” observation is that
        there is a common multiplicative
        factor $$\gamma$$ separating $A$, from $\sqrt{AB}$,
        from $B$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_construction_1_v2.svg

        The proof is that

        $$
        {\sqrt{AB}\over A} = {B\over\sqrt{AB}}
        $$

        is equivalent to

        $$
        (\sqrt{AB})^2 = AB
        $$

        which is true
        by definition of $\sqrt{AB}$—or a second proof
        (we can't help ourselves) is that...

        $$
        {\sqrt{AB}\over A} = {\sqrt{A}\cdot\sqrt{B}\over A} = {\sqrt{B}\over \sqrt{A}}
        $$

        ...while...

        $$
        {B\over \sqrt{AB}} = {B \over \sqrt{A}\cdot\sqrt{B}} = {\sqrt{B}\over \sqrt{A}}
        $$

        ...is equal, and we can explicitly see that $\gamma = \sqrt{B}/\sqrt{A}$, as
        well, from the second proof. (Indeed, if you multiply
        twice by $\sqrt{B}/\sqrt{A}$ you're going to remove one
        factor of $A$ while introducing one factor of $B$.)
        
        Now:

        |> Pause

        _Solution 1._ Assume wlog (= “without loss of generality”)
        that $B > A$. We report the lengths $A$ and $B$ onto a
        common number line such that the left end of length $B$
        is identified with $0$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_construction_2.svg

        We construct $B/2$ by intersecting two sufficiently
        large circles of centers $0$, $B$, of equal radius:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_construction_3_v2.svg

        We draw a half-circle of radius $B/2$ and of center $B/2$,
        and a perpendicular emanating from $A$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_construction_4.svg

        This red triangle is a right triangle by the
        Peloponnesian circle theorem:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_construction_5.svg

        So we have succeeded in constructing this diagram,
        for some angle $\theta$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_construction_6.svg

        As seen in Exercise >>_ratio_A_over_B_1_+_tan^2_,
        however, we have

        $$
        {B\over A} = \sec^2\theta
        $$

        in this case, implying that

        $$
        \sec\theta = {\sqrt{B}\over\sqrt{A}}
        $$

        is the factor $\gamma$ mentioned in the
        !! at the beginning of the solution
        preliminary arithmetic observation, and that

        $$
        A\sec\theta = A\gamma = \sqrt{AB}
        $$

        which places $\sqrt{AB}$...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_construction_7.svg

        ...here, in our construction from a few lines
        above.

        |> Pause

        _Solution 2._
        Place $A$ and $B$ end-to-end:
        !! and draw a circle
        !! whose diameter is the combined segment:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_solution2_0.svg

        Find the middle of the combined segment and
        use it to
        draw a semicircle whose diameter is the
        combined segment:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_solution2_1.svg

        Draw a perpendicular
        to the segment at the point where $A$ and $B$
        meet, which defines again a right triangle by the
        Peloponnesian theorem:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_solution2_2.svg

        Because 
        of the right angle,
        the angle $\theta$ at lower left—if we label it
        such—is also repeated middle top right (this is
        the content of Exercise >>_complement_of_complement_):

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_solution2_3.svg

        The ratio that takes $A$ to the middle height
        and that takes the middle height to $B$ are therefore
        both $\tan(\theta)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_sqrt_AB_solution2_4.svg

        This implies that

        $$
        \tan(\theta)
        $$

        is the factor $\gamma$ such that $A\gamma^2 = B$
        mentioned at the outset, and that said “middle
        height” is $\sqrt{AB}$!

        |> Pause

        _Vocabulary._ The square-root-of-the-product

        $$
        \sqrt{xy}
        $$

        is called the __geometric mean__ of $x$ and $y$,
        $x \geq 0$, $y \geq 0$, as opposed to

        $$
        {x + y\over 2}
        $$

        which is called the

        __arithmetic mean__

        (or just “mean”) of $x$ and $y$, $x \in \rr$, $y \in \rr$.

        |> SolutionNote

            Legend has it that a

            __geometric progression__

            is so-called because each term 
            in the sequence
            that is not the first or the last is the
            geometric mean of the two terms on either side,
            and that similarly an

            __arithmetic progression__

            is so-called because each term
            in the sequence
            that is not the first or the last is the
            arithematic mean of the two terms on either side.
            (Cf. Note >>_geometric_progression_ in Exercise >>_1d_red_particle_
            of Chapter >>derivatives.)